<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">São Luís, Maranhão</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-luis">São Luís</a> is the capital of the Brazilian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maranhao">Maranhão</a>, on an island off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>'s northern coast. It grew from Fort Saint-Louis, built in 1612 by the French colonists of Equinoctial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and named after Louis XIII. The Portuguese captured it in 1615, and it became the centre of the State of Maranhão. Its colonial centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sao-luis-maranhao.jpg" alt="São Luís, Maranhão" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lechenet.com · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A3o_Lu%C3%ADs_A%C3%A9rea.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
